(5476541, 779010, 67697, '<td valign="top">I love how you included the ball just about to leave the frame.  It''s amazing how pitchers can contort their bodies the way they do.  Great catch!</td>', 'I love how you included the ball just about to leave the frame.  It''s amazing how pitchers can contort their bodies the way they do.  Great catch!', '2009-05-08 10:30:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5476546, 786834, 98339, '<td valign="top">what an amazing shot...I gave it a 10....</td>', 'what an amazing shot...I gave it a 10....', '2009-05-08 10:32:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(5476547, 782345, 80908, '<td valign="top">A lot of this will be down to taste, but for me the benefit of HDR is the chance to expose more of the picture correctly, while maintaining the look of a photo. I have seen HDRI used very effectively to produce a slightly ''cartoon'' image as you have here but it leaves me a little cold. <br/> <br/>I would also recommend processing to avoid the halos around the chimney (a common sign of excessive use of the shadow/highlight tool as well I have found) and the slightly over saturated colours.<br/> <br/>To me it actually doesn''t look that crisp - the lines are sharp but thick, which I often also find a problem when I am trying HDR and my photos are not exactly in line (from manual rather than remote shutter normally!)<br/> <br/>As to why it didn''t score well, I just don''t think it is to DPC taste. If you look at the front page, the photos tend to be sunsets/landscapes or action shots of this and that. Do one of those and you can get away with all sorts of technical failures (mentioning no names on a recent challenge!)</td>', 'A lot of this will be down to taste, but for me the benefit of HDR is the chance to expose more of the picture correctly, while maintaining the look of a photo. I have seen HDRI used very effectively to produce a slightly ''cartoon'' image as you have here but it leaves me a little cold.  I would also recommend processing to avoid the halos around the chimney (a common sign of excessive use of the shadow/highlight tool as well I have found) and the slightly over saturated colours. To me it actually doesn''t look that crisp - the lines are sharp but thick, which I often also find a problem when I am trying HDR and my photos are not exactly in line (from manual rather than remote shutter normally!) As to why it didn''t score well, I just don''t think it is to DPC taste. If you look at the front page, the photos tend to be sunsets/landscapes or action shots of this and that. Do one of those and you can get away with all sorts of technical failures (mentioning no names on a recent challenge!)', '2009-05-08 10:33:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(5476553, 786940, 67152, '<td valign="top">Judy- Since you are one of the prime reasons I began to leave comments, I decided to find your last entry and leave my 9000th comment.  Your comments are always a joy to read, thoughtful, helpful, providing both critique and encouragement.<br/> <br/>I quite like this little poppy shot.  Elements I espeically like are the edges being so sharp, the colors and petals soft and delicate, and the flower almost wilting as if the black is pulling their life away.  Too subtle to score well, but lovely all the way.<br/></td>', 'Judy- Since you are one of the prime reasons I began to leave comments, I decided to find your last entry and leave my 9000th comment.  Your comments are always a joy to read, thoughtful, helpful, providing both critique and encouragement. I quite like this little poppy shot.  Elements I espeically like are the edges being so sharp, the colors and petals soft and delicate, and the flower almost wilting as if the black is pulling their life away.  Too subtle to score well, but lovely all the way.', '2009-05-08 10:35:23.000000', NULL, 0),
